  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThursday through Sunday\nAssessment...high (>80%) confidence of warming temperatures and\nhigher humidity for the weekend.\n\nThe late week and weekend will see rising temperatures and humidity \nlevels, especially over the weekend. Dew points will be in the mid \nto upper 60s on Saturday making the humidity noticeable.\n\nSunday dew points in the upper 60s to lower 70s should be common. \nThe possibility does exist that pockets of mid 70s dew points could \nform. When combined with probable highs in the upper 80s to lower \n90s, heat indices of 95 to 100 would be widespread.\n\nThe model consensus continues to advertise rain late Thursday night \nand mainly Friday morning. The rain chances are 15 to 50 percent for \nareas south of I-80. The overall consensus is biased toward the \nwetter GFS and many of its ensemble members.\n\nThe other global models are mainly dry with their respective \ndeterministic runs. HOWEVER, there are ensemble members from each \nglobal model (much fewer than the GFS) that bring rain into the \narea. The vacillating of how far north the rain gets with each \nmodel cycle appears to be tied to the strength of the high over the \nwestern Great Lakes. With building heights aloft in the Plains, the \nentire system and rain should be further south across Missouri.\n\nSunday night through Tuesday\nAssessment...medium (50-70%) confidence on above normal temperatures\n\nHeights aloft will continue building north in the Plains the first \npart of next week. The question is will the area be in the active \nzone for organized nocturnal storm complexes. Thermal and flow \nprofiles currently suggest the organized storm complexes would be \nMinnesota/Wisconsin and into Michigan.\n\nWith the summer monsoon season ongoing in the desert southwest there \nwill be numerous disturbances moving around the edge of the heat \ndome to produce organized storm complexes. The model consensus does \nhave 20-30 percent rain chances with two separate disturbances \nSunday night/Monday and again Monday night. I suspect those chances \nwill slowly drop the remainder of the week.",
